Rimshot the Orcish Rogue has gone successfully to his reward. As always, by
the time I finished, the early game was pretty much a blur. Did find
several helpful bones piles, some of mine own, some off of Hearse. I got
Stormbringer off one of my old piles and used it for the rest of the game.
It worked pretty well, although I killed several things I didn't mean to.
(I normally don't touch shopkeepers.) Lost pet early on and never bothered
to replace. Used rings of polymorph and polymorph control to do the Quest,
and had little trouble with the Master Assassin. Had one bad spot later on
where three arch-liches were ganging up on me all at once around a
staircase; fortunately I got a rubber chicken and solved that problem.

Didn't get anything off any throne, except for one case of blindness.

I suppose someone will comment on my relatively paltry armor collection;
the RNG was really stingy with magic markers (two in the whole game) and I
don't like polymorphing objects. I suppose I should have used one more wish
and gotten another marker, but I got by with what I had. Or maybe a helm of
brilliance would have been better; Orcs are Stoopid, and not having the
helm cost me on the planes when my crystal ball blew up in my face.

Spent two wishes from magic lamps (more bones file goodies) to get a silver
dagger and a scroll of charging. (How's that for pathetic?) The castle wand
is the one I was carrying at the end, and the only one I found in the game.
My final experience level was high for me and I only pushed it that far so
I could be an expert in two-weapon combat; let too many skill slots get
filled earlier on. 

The final charge was pretty easy. The only one to really hit me hard was
the High Priestess in the Sanctum, with her "gaping wounds" attack. (Is
there any way to prevent that?) Blew through the planes, cut down Famine
with a Wand of Death, and the correct altar was the first one I tried.

I enjoyed playing a rogue. Once you become an Expert at it, you can really
soften up (if not kill) enemies with a dagger-missile attack. Having
automatic searching was great, and having your Quest Leader treat you like
dirt actually made for a nice change of pace. Even without the helm, I had
pretty good spellcasting powers, once I got a robe.


